{
  "schemaVersion": 4,
  "id": "t02-020",
  "track": "track-02",
  "title": "Kestrel endpoints TLS reverse proxies and IIS integration",
  "status": "source-backed-scope-complete",
  "inventoryComplete": true,
  "concepts": [
    "Kestrel is the cross-platform ASP.NET Core HTTP server. Endpoint binding, TLS termination, proxy forwarding, and request limits are separate layers: configure each at the layer that owns it and understand which server is directly receiving a connection. URL settings are convenient defaults, while explicit Listen endpoints provide lower-level control.",
    "When an app runs behind a reverse proxy or load balancer, the proxy may terminate TLS and forward the original scheme, host, and client address in headers. Those headers are untrusted input unless the immediate proxy is trusted and the forwarded-header middleware is configured accordingly. The middleware must run early enough for redirects, authentication, link generation, and HSTS to use the original request information.",
    "IIS can host ASP.NET Core in-process using IISHttpServer or out-of-process with Kestrel behind the ASP.NET Core Module. The selected hosting model changes the process boundary, server implementation, and diagnostics. IIS hosting integration is not the same as Kestrel reverse-proxy hosting; use the deployment track for detailed IIS and proxy operations.",
    "TLS certificates and request limits are security and capacity controls. Do not hardcode production certificate passwords, trust arbitrary forwarded headers, or copy example connection caps without workload evidence. Keep transport configuration and deployment secrets outside source-controlled app code."
  ],
