  "versionNotes": [
    {
      "edition": "ASP.NET Core 11 / .NET 11 GA checked 2026-11-20",
      "items": [
        "Starting in ASP.NET Core 8.0.17 and 9.0.6, forwarded headers from unknown proxies are ignored unless the sender is configured in KnownProxies or KnownNetworks. In ASP.NET Core 10, the framework's custom IPNetwork and KnownNetworks APIs became obsolete in favor of System.Net.IPNetwork and ForwardedHeadersOptions.KnownIPNetworks; teach the current API and label KnownNetworks as migration context.",
        "ASP.NET Core 11 Kestrel starts processing HTTP/3 requests before waiting for the QUIC control stream and SETTINGS frame, reducing first-request latency on new connections.",
        "ASP.NET Core 11 adds Kestrel TLS handshake diagnostics through ITlsHandshakeFeature.Exception, replaces the old TlsClientHelloBytesCallback customization shape with ListenOptions.UseTlsClientHelloListener, applies RequestHeadersTimeout to incomplete HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 trailer headers, and exposes TLS channel-binding bytes through ITlsConnectionFeature.TryGetChannelBindingBytes.",
        "In-process IIS hosting uses IISHttpServer, while out-of-process hosting places Kestrel behind ANCM. The `AspNetCoreHostingModel` project property controls the deployment hosting model; don't describe both modes as Kestrel hosting.",
        "Kestrel, IIS/ANCM, and reverse-proxy products have independently updated APIs and limits. Avoid claiming a universal request or connection limit; use the current official reference and deployment-specific configuration.",
        "Current Kestrel security and API references enumerate request-line/header/buffer limits and protocol-specific Http2Limits/Http3Limits in addition to body, connection, timeout, and data-rate limits. Keep defaults explicit as target-version values.",
        "ASP.NET Core 11 documentation marks IWebHostBuilder.UseQuic/QuicTransportOptions as preview; avoid treating the lower-level QUIC tuning API as a stable general-purpose configuration surface.",
        "Current IIS ANCM defaults to in-process hosting when no hosting model is specified. For out-of-process hosting, IIS owns the request-body cap and Kestrel MaxRequestBodySize is disabled.",
        "ASPNETCORE_FORWARDEDHEADERS_ENABLED enables X-Forwarded-For and X-Forwarded-Proto while clearing the known-proxy/network lists; it is a broad trust configuration, not an allowlist.",
        "HTTP/3 ServerCertificateSelector receives null ConnectionContext",
        "HTTP/3 UseHttps HttpsConnectionAdapterOptions HandshakeTimeout and OnAuthenticate are no-op",
        "HTTP/3 UseHttps ServerOptionsSelectionCallback and TlsHandshakeCallbackOptions overloads throw"
      ],
